WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.380 --> 00:00:04.590
Danny Trejo's rise to fame had quite a few
twists and turns along the way.

00:00:04.590 --> 00:00:08.780
Years before he took up acting, he was actually
a violent criminal who'd spent time in multiple

00:00:08.780 --> 00:00:09.280
prisons.

00:00:09.400 --> 00:00:12.100
So how was he able to turn his life around
so drastically?

00:00:12.680 --> 00:00:17.159
Long before showing up in a Snickers commercial
as Marcia Brady, Danny Trejo was on the streets

00:00:17.160 --> 00:00:20.920
of Los Angeles, getting started in the criminal
business when he was just a kid.

00:00:21.269 --> 00:00:25.120
He recalls that he was only 12 when he was
first tossed into juvenile, for the crime

00:00:25.120 --> 00:00:26.700
of hitting another kid with a rock.

00:00:27.040 --> 00:00:31.160
Things only escalated from there, largely
thanks to his Uncle Gilbert, who would rob

00:00:31.160 --> 00:00:33.880
stores and have his nephew Danny act as the
getaway driver.

00:00:34.500 --> 00:00:38.360
He would go on to commit at least five armed
robberies before he was busted for selling

00:00:38.360 --> 00:00:41.160
a four-ounce bag of heroin worth $30,000.

00:00:41.780 --> 00:00:46.560
As a result, the 23-year-old Trejo was sentenced
to ten years and shipped off to San Quentin

00:00:46.560 --> 00:00:49.220
where he quickly became one of the baddest
dudes behind bars.

00:00:49.980 --> 00:00:54.360
After Trejo arrived in San Quentin, he quickly
made a name for himself by donning a pair

00:00:54.360 --> 00:00:56.200
of boxing gloves and knocking bums out.

00:00:56.540 --> 00:01:00.840
He claims that prison boxing, quote, "literally
made [him] a celebrity," and that he was both

00:01:00.840 --> 00:01:03.760
the lightweight and welterweight champ in
three separate prisons.

00:01:04.040 --> 00:01:06.060
And he was just as scary outside the ring.

00:01:06.340 --> 00:01:10.960
As the head of a small gang, he made quite
a bit of cash by intimidating weaker inmates,

00:01:10.960 --> 00:01:12.680
forcing them to fork over protection money.

00:01:13.369 --> 00:01:17.820
Naturally, most people agreed to pay up, because
who's going to say no to Danny Trejo?

00:01:17.820 --> 00:01:21.320
It was also behind bars that Trejo picked
up his infamous tattoo.

00:01:21.829 --> 00:01:26.100
If you've ever seen the man shirtless, you've
probably noticed the sombrero-wearing, cleavage-baring

00:01:26.100 --> 00:01:27.860
cowgirl scrawled across his chest.

00:01:28.220 --> 00:01:32.280
So how did Danny Trejo go from terrifying
thug to beloved public figure?

00:01:32.280 --> 00:01:36.660
His transformation began on Cinco de Mayo
1968 when he decided to riot.

00:01:36.969 --> 00:01:41.460
It all started after some of the Mexican inmates
got a bit inebriated, and soon, the prison

00:01:41.460 --> 00:01:42.800
had erupted into madness.

00:01:43.220 --> 00:01:47.180
In the chaos, Trejo hit a prison official
in the head with a rock, a crime that got

00:01:47.180 --> 00:01:48.940
him tossed into solitary confinement.

00:01:49.300 --> 00:01:53.620
Trejo spent over three months in solitary,
acting out movies like The Wizard of Oz and

00:01:53.620 --> 00:01:55.640
The Hunchback of Notre Dame to keep sane.

00:01:56.120 --> 00:01:59.959
Realizing he needed to get his life straightened
out, he started to pray, saying,

00:01:59.960 --> 00:02:04.009
"God if you're there, everything will be alright,
if you're not, I'm screwed."

00:02:04.280 --> 00:02:08.700
Miraculously, the charges against him were
dropped, and he slowly but surely began to

00:02:08.700 --> 00:02:09.900
rehabilitate himself.

00:02:10.220 --> 00:02:13.580
Trejo also turned his life around by knuckling
down and getting sober.

00:02:13.740 --> 00:02:19.080
He started using marijuana at age eight and
shooting heroin at age 12, and he also consumed

00:02:19.080 --> 00:02:21.040
a whole lot of alcohol in his early days.

00:02:21.280 --> 00:02:25.550
Determined to set things right, he joined
Alcoholics Anonymous and Narcotics Anonymous,

00:02:25.550 --> 00:02:28.520
and today, he has been sober for nearly 50
years.

00:02:28.750 --> 00:02:33.040
And after he was released from prison, he
decided to lend a hand to his fellow man,

00:02:33.050 --> 00:02:35.360
working as a drug counselor for 15 years.

00:02:35.360 --> 00:02:38.760
Today, he is still counseling people and speaking
to at-risk youth.

00:02:39.300 --> 00:02:44.560
Trejo kept himself pretty busy after leaving
prison in 1969, working as both a drug counselor

00:02:44.560 --> 00:02:45.740
and court liaison.

00:02:46.200 --> 00:02:48.840
But he was forced to do all sorts of things
to pay the bills.

00:02:49.220 --> 00:02:53.080
He tried becoming a boxer, but when his license
was denied, he picked up some pocket change

00:02:53.080 --> 00:02:56.800
by throwing down in "smokers," aka unsanctioned
matches.

00:02:57.140 --> 00:03:01.260
He also earned a living by working at a wrecking
yard before teaming up with a friend to start

00:03:01.260 --> 00:03:02.560
his very own enterprise.

00:03:02.870 --> 00:03:07.400
The two opened a gardening business, but while
they had plenty of get-up-and-go, they didn't

00:03:07.400 --> 00:03:08.960
have all the necessary equipment.

00:03:09.320 --> 00:03:10.700
They didn't even have a lawnmower.

00:03:10.880 --> 00:03:14.220
However, Trejo's tough times were soon all
about to change.

00:03:14.840 --> 00:03:19.460
In 1985, Trejo was working as a drug counselor
when he got a call from a client who desperately

00:03:19.460 --> 00:03:20.120
needed help.

00:03:20.680 --> 00:03:24.920
This guy was working on the set of Runaway
Train, a prison thriller starring Jon Voight

00:03:24.920 --> 00:03:28.280
and Eric Roberts, and he was struggling with
an urge to use cocaine.

00:03:28.480 --> 00:03:32.500
Trejo showed up on the set to give the guy
some assistance, but it wasn't long before

00:03:32.500 --> 00:03:35.760
someone approached Trejo and asked if he'd
like to be an extra in the movie.

00:03:35.960 --> 00:03:40.400
He took the job, but as he was changing into
his outfit, screenwriter Eddie Bunker spotted

00:03:40.400 --> 00:03:41.360
his chest tattoo.

00:03:41.860 --> 00:03:46.580
Bunker had also served time, and when he saw
the cowgirl ink, he remembered Trejo's boxing

00:03:46.580 --> 00:03:47.400
behind bars.

00:03:47.970 --> 00:03:51.880
It was an amazing coincidence, especially
since the filmmakers were looking for someone

00:03:51.880 --> 00:03:53.520
to teach Eric Roberts how to fight.

00:03:53.820 --> 00:03:54.820
“I said, what’s it pay?

00:03:55.200 --> 00:03:56.260
And he said, $320 a day.”

00:03:56.660 --> 00:04:00.220
Trejo gave the actor a few pointers, which
so impressed the director that he offered

00:04:00.220 --> 00:04:04.080
Trejo a much bigger part as the guy who squares
off against Roberts on-screen.

00:04:04.080 --> 00:04:05.710
“And then I remember he kissed me.

00:04:05.710 --> 00:04:08.300
He kissed me on this cheek and a kiss on..

00:04:08.300 --> 00:04:09.620
Andrei Konchalovsky, a Russian”

00:04:09.620 --> 00:04:13.540
“And I remember telling Eddie Bunker, I
said ‘Eddie, look, I’m gonna do this training

00:04:13.540 --> 00:04:17.739
for $320 but if I’m gonna be kissing this
old man I want more money.”

00:04:17.880 --> 00:04:23.140
It was Trejo's first role, the one that kickstarted
his entire acting career, and it was all thanks

00:04:23.140 --> 00:04:24.280
to that wild tattoo.

00:04:25.040 --> 00:04:28.200
Ever since Runaway Train, Trejo has been keeping
himself pretty busy.

00:04:28.600 --> 00:04:32.680
Visit his IMDb page, and you'll find he's
got over 300 credits in his filmography.

00:04:33.180 --> 00:04:37.380
During his 30 plus years of acting, he's starred
alongside some of the biggest action stars

00:04:37.389 --> 00:04:40.620
in the business, like Charles Bronson, Steven
Seagal, and Chuck Norris.

00:04:40.620 --> 00:04:43.600
Trejo has also appeared in some of the biggest
thrillers of all time.

00:04:43.910 --> 00:04:47.980
He played "Johnny 23" in Con Air, a movie
he once described as "the biggest case of

00:04:47.980 --> 00:04:49.560
testosterone I've ever been in."

00:04:49.980 --> 00:04:54.470
He also showed up in the Michael Mann classic
Heat as the appropriately named Trejo, a member

00:04:54.470 --> 00:04:56.060
of Robert De Niro's criminal crew.

00:04:56.520 --> 00:05:01.560
Other notable credits include xXx, Six Days,
Seven Nights, Anaconda, and The Devil's Rejects.

00:05:01.760 --> 00:05:06.040
Plus, if you've ever turned on a TV, you've
no doubt seen Trejo in shows like The X-Files,

00:05:06.040 --> 00:05:10.820
Baywatch, Sons of Anarchy, Breaking Bad, and
even the soap opera The Young and the Restless.

00:05:11.100 --> 00:05:15.660
In addition to action flicks, he's also done
kid movies like Muppets Most Wanted and comedies

00:05:15.660 --> 00:05:16.660
like Anchorman.

00:05:16.900 --> 00:05:18.540
"You're gonna have to learn how to deal with
that."

00:05:19.920 --> 00:05:20.420
"What?

00:05:20.720 --> 00:05:21.440
Were you saying something?

00:05:22.380 --> 00:05:23.600
Look, I don't speak Spanish"

00:05:24.040 --> 00:05:28.460
Cinema is full of fantastic actor-director
collaborations, and Danny Trejo and Robert

00:05:28.460 --> 00:05:32.080
Rodriguez deserve to be considered among the
most legendary pairs.

00:05:32.080 --> 00:05:36.620
The two first met while working on Desperado,
where Rodriguez was struck by Trejo's "natural

00:05:36.620 --> 00:05:37.280
magnetism."

00:05:37.710 --> 00:05:41.580
Strangely enough, while working on the film
they discovered that they are actually second

00:05:41.580 --> 00:05:42.080
cousins.

00:05:42.320 --> 00:05:44.740
"Where do these ideas, crazy ideas come from?

00:05:44.749 --> 00:05:46.840
And what line- It's all him, right?"

00:05:46.840 --> 00:05:50.330
After their first film, the two quickly formed
a personal and professional bond that would

00:05:50.330 --> 00:05:54.080
lead to nine movies, including Spy Kids and
From Dusk Till Dawn.

00:05:54.440 --> 00:05:59.100
But it's safe to say the two are best known
for working on Machete, the 2010 exploitation

00:05:59.100 --> 00:06:02.320
flick that definitively made Danny Trejo a
household name.

00:06:12.180 --> 00:06:16.400
Rodriguez was inspired to make this nutso
B-picture from pretty much the first moment

00:06:16.400 --> 00:06:17.520
he laid eyes on Trejo.

00:06:17.990 --> 00:06:22.960
He envisioned his star as the, quote, "Mexican
Jean-Claude Van Damme or Charles Bronson,"

00:06:22.960 --> 00:06:26.740
and thus he transformed into the machete-wielding
vigilante we now know and love.

00:06:28.040 --> 00:06:29.520
"You could've at least texted me"

00:06:31.700 --> 00:06:32.800
"Machete don't text"

00:06:33.640 --> 00:06:36.900
Once you've become the toughest star in Hollywood,
where do you go next?

00:06:37.140 --> 00:06:39.460
If you're Trejo, you go into the restaurant
business.

00:06:39.840 --> 00:06:44.170
With the help of two partners, the action
star recently founded his own culinary kingdom,

00:06:44.170 --> 00:06:45.900
with locations across Los Angeles.

00:06:46.020 --> 00:06:49.460
If you're in the mood for Mexican cuisine,
you can stop by Trejo's Tacos.

00:06:50.020 --> 00:06:53.280
If you want coffee and churros, you can drop
in to Trejo's Coffee &amp; Donuts.

00:06:53.760 --> 00:06:57.940
And if you fancy a proper drink with your
meal, you can swing by Trejo's Cantinas.

00:06:58.080 --> 00:07:01.080
Trejo's restaurants have vegan, vegetarian,
and gluten-free options.

00:07:01.540 --> 00:07:05.680
And the actor makes sure that nothing goes
to waste, with all leftovers taken to a homeless

00:07:05.680 --> 00:07:06.180
shelter.

00:07:06.500 --> 00:07:10.860
Trejo's food is so good that it was featured
on Anthony Bourdain's show, Parts Unknown,

00:07:10.860 --> 00:07:13.860
and the late, great chef had nothing but praise
for the Mexican cuisine.

00:07:14.320 --> 00:07:18.020
If you live outside California, you might
have to wait a bit before you can buy yourself

00:07:18.020 --> 00:07:22.800
a "Lowrider" donut, but the actor has plans
to open locations in San Antonio, Las Vegas,

00:07:22.800 --> 00:07:23.300
and New York.

00:07:23.920 --> 00:07:28.120
Everybody knows that under his intimidating
exterior, Danny Trejo is a big ol' softie.

00:07:28.880 --> 00:07:33.060
He's a dad, a drug counselor, a dude who gives
food to the homeless, and naturally, he's

00:07:33.060 --> 00:07:34.520
also a big-time dog lover.

00:07:34.920 --> 00:07:38.880
In fact, he's such a fan of our four-legged
friends that he's dedicated countless hours

00:07:38.880 --> 00:07:41.080
to making the world a safer place for pets.

00:07:41.370 --> 00:07:46.320
With his ex-wife Debbie, Trejo founded a group
called K9 Compassion, a rescue organization

00:07:46.320 --> 00:07:48.780
that emphasizes the importance of spaying
and neutering.

00:07:48.860 --> 00:07:53.250
The actor has worked with various groups like
Best Friends Animal Society and the Villalobos

00:07:53.250 --> 00:07:57.320
Rescue Center, he's spoken with high school
students about proper pet care, and he's appeared

00:07:57.320 --> 00:07:59.060
in ads for animal foundations.

00:07:59.300 --> 00:08:02.460
And like any dedicated dog lover, Trejo believes
in adoption.

00:08:02.860 --> 00:08:05.820
As of 2016, had four cute canines of his own.

00:08:06.600 --> 00:08:10.960
Even though Trejo is one macho man, he has
his limits when it comes to screen action.

00:08:11.420 --> 00:08:14.820
In fact, he's very opinionated when it comes
to the topic of stunt work.

00:08:15.000 --> 00:08:19.540
After Tom Cruise injured himself filming Mission:
Impossible, Fallout, Trejo told Yahoo!

00:08:19.540 --> 00:08:20.040
Movies,

00:08:20.380 --> 00:08:24.100
"I know that all the big stars hate me to
say this, but I don't want to risk 80 peoples'

00:08:24.100 --> 00:08:27.160
jobs just to say I got big huevos on The Tonight
Show."

00:08:27.520 --> 00:08:31.570
According to Trejo, it's irresponsible for
a leading man or woman to jeopardize a film

00:08:31.570 --> 00:08:35.320
shoot in order to crawl up buildings or hold
onto the side of planes.

00:08:35.610 --> 00:08:39.680
If the lead gets injured, that might ruin
the entire film, and then everybody loses

00:08:39.680 --> 00:08:40.420
a paycheck.

00:08:40.780 --> 00:08:44.540
Danny Trejo might seem like a scary guy on
the big screen, but in reality, he has a heart

00:08:44.540 --> 00:08:45.040
of gold.

00:08:45.260 --> 00:08:49.260
Knowing full well that he packs some serious
celebrity clout, he does his best to make

00:08:49.260 --> 00:08:50.580
a difference wherever he goes.

00:08:51.220 --> 00:08:55.020
That means working as a drug intervention
counselor, fighting for autism awareness,

00:08:55.020 --> 00:08:59.720
and appearing in PSAs to promote good manners
on the L.A. Metro and to support male caregivers.

00:09:00.380 --> 00:09:05.740
Trejo is also incredibly serious about prison
reform, going so far as to produce a 2018

00:09:05.740 --> 00:09:08.100
documentary called Survivor's Guide to Prison.

00:09:08.440 --> 00:09:12.960
Trejo also appears in the film to analyze
the problems with the U.S. legal system, talk

00:09:12.970 --> 00:09:16.960
about the abuse that goes on behind bars,
and discuss the cases of two men who were

00:09:16.960 --> 00:09:18.980
wrongly convicted and incarcerated.

00:09:19.330 --> 00:09:23.540
As a guy who's been on the inside, he knows
a thing or two about how cruel prison can

00:09:23.540 --> 00:09:28.040
be, and how authority figures are often indifferent
to the inmates struggling to survive.

00:09:28.360 --> 00:09:30.940
Danny Trejo is a man who likes to keep busy.

00:09:30.940 --> 00:09:33.580
And he does a whole lot more than just appear
on the silver screen.

00:09:33.780 --> 00:09:36.680
For example, he's provided voices for multiple
video games.

00:09:37.060 --> 00:09:40.040
He also recently promoted the online shooter
Guns of Boom.

00:09:40.580 --> 00:09:42.980
Trejo's also been a notable star of music
videos.

00:09:43.420 --> 00:09:48.000
He goes full-on Psycho in Slayer's "Pride
in Prejudice," and throws back some shots

00:09:48.000 --> 00:09:49.580
in Enrique Iglesias' "Loco."

00:09:50.100 --> 00:09:53.980
He gets pushed around by the Jonas Brothers
in "Burnin' Up and gives off serious Machete

00:09:53.980 --> 00:09:56.020
vibes in Train's "Angel in Blue Jeans."

00:09:56.290 --> 00:09:59.900
In other words, you don't have to look very
far to find Danny Trejo...because he's pretty

00:09:59.900 --> 00:10:00.640
much everywhere.

